<?xpacket begin="ï»¿" id="W5M0MpCehiHzreSzNTczkc9d"?>
<x:xmpmeta xmlns:x="adobe:ns:meta/">
  <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#">
    <rdf:Description xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" rdf:about="">
      <dc:title>
        <rdf:Alt>
          <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Becoming Adora</rdf:li>
        </rdf:Alt>
      </dc:title>
      <dc:description>
        <rdf:Alt>
          <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">&lt;p&gt;Coming out is difficult. To yourself, and to everyone else.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Itâ€™s even more difficult when you finally find the word that describes you perfectly, a word that is the key to figuring out who you are, but itâ€™s one that carries a lot of negative implications. A word that means that many people will outright hate you for simply existing and will actively make your life harder. Itâ€™s a difficult pill to swallow, knowing that your life will be significantly more challenging despite having done nothing wrong, nothing to deserve this. If only it was &lt;i&gt;something&lt;/i&gt; else, &lt;i&gt;anything&lt;/i&gt; elseâ€¦&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Itâ€™s a pill that sometimes seems easier to ignore. To repress, to hide away the shame, to pretend to be the perfect son who got good grades, obeyed his parents, and earned the rank of Eagle Scout, someone to be proud of. Because as horrendous as an experience puberty is, destroying her hopes and dreams in a slow-moving train wreck that showed no signs of stopping, the fear of how others would react seemed far worse.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;How long can it last?&lt;/p&gt;</rdf:li>
        </rdf:Alt>
      </dc:description>
      <dc:creator>
        <rdf:Seq>
          <rdf:li>peachpeachpeachpeachpeach</rdf:li>
        </rdf:Seq>
      </dc:creator>
